{number: 58}
{title: I Love to Tell the Story}
{key: Ab}

{start_of_verse: 1}
[Ab] I love to tell the story of [Db] unseen things a-[Ab] bove,
Of Jesus and His glory, of [Eb7] Jesus and His [Ab] love.
I love to tell the story, be-[Db] cause I know 'tis [Ab] true;
It satisfies my longings as [Eb7] nothing else could [Ab] do.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
I [Eb7] love to tell the [Ab] story,
'Twill [Db] be my theme in [Ab] glory,
To [Db] tell the old, old [Ab] story
Of [Eb7] Jesus and His [Ab] love.
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse: 2}
[Ab] I love to tell the story; more [Db] wonderful it [Ab] seems
Than all the golden fancies of [Eb7] all our golden [Ab] dreams.
I love to tell the story, it [Db] did so much for [Ab] me;
And that is just the reason I [Eb7] tell it now to [Ab] thee.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 3}
[Ab] I love to tell the story; 'tis [Db] pleasant to re-[Ab] peat
What seems, each time I tell it, more [Eb7] wonderfully [Ab] sweet.
I love to tell the story, for [Db] some have never [Ab] heard
The message of salvation from [Eb7] God's own holy [Ab] Word.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: 4}
[Ab] I love to tell the story, for [Db] those who know it [Ab] best
Seem hungering and thirsting to [Eb7] hear it like the [Ab] rest.
And when, in scenes of glory, I [Db] sing the new, new [Ab] song,
'Twill be the old, old story that [Eb7] I have loved so [Ab] long.
{end_of_verse}
